course: dictionary
lesson: 5082
title: Understanding insurance
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Insurance is a deal with a company. You pay them a small amount regularly, and if something bad happens, like an accident, theft or illness, the company helps to pay. «Countries also trade services, such as banking, insurance, software, films and tourism, not only goods.» Here insurance is a service, something a company provides, not something you can pick up and hold.
explain: Context clues help you understand it. «A burglary is when someone breaks into a home to steal; victims are often upset most by feeling unsafe, and insurance can pay for some of what was taken.» The words pay for some of what was taken tell you that insurance is money that helps after a loss.
explain: Insurance can also mean any protection against something going wrong. «I took an umbrella as insurance.» Nobody bought an insurance deal here. The speaker just protected themselves against rain. Check by swapping in your meaning: "I took an umbrella as protection against rain" still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Look for words that often go together, such as take out insurance, an insurance policy and an insurance claim. Noticing these phrases helps you remember what the word means and how to use it.
fact: Insurance means paying a company a small amount regularly, so that they help if something bad happens.
fact: Insurance is uncountable, so you say some insurance, not an insurance, when you mean the idea in general.
fact: Common types include car insurance, house insurance, travel insurance and health insurance.
fact: An insurance policy is the agreement that sets out what the company will pay for.
fact: «Insurance» can also mean any protection against something going wrong, as in «as insurance against rain».
exercise: What is insurance? | *A regular small payment to a company that helps if something bad happens | A large gift from a bank that never has to be paid back | A tool for fixing cars | A kind of bank account with no fees
exercise: «Countries also trade services, such as banking, insurance, software, films and tourism.» Which of these is a service, not a good? | A bicycle | A jar of jam | *Insurance | A loaf of bread
exercise: «I took an umbrella as insurance.» What does insurance mean here? | *Protection against something going wrong, like rain | A holiday to a wet place | A paid job at a rain shop | A kind of umbrella with a long handle
test: After a «burglary», what can insurance help with? | The weather forecast for the week | *Some of what was taken | The burglar's trial in court
test: Which phrase means you buy insurance? | Lose the insurance | *Take out insurance | Drop the insurance
exam: Which sentence uses insurance to mean any protection against something going wrong? | *I took an umbrella as insurance | The insurance company paid the claim | The insurance policy was kept in the drawer | I pay for car insurance each month
